Before the code: a purely canvas-drawn input is invisible to screen readers, and it also breaks autofill, password managers, IME, text selection, mobile keyboards, and forced-colors mode. It would fail your contract requirement.

The approach that keeps your branding and the requirement is to **keep a real `<input>` and use the canvas only as the visual layer.** The input sits on top at `opacity: 0` (not `display:none` or `visibility:hidden`, which would remove it from the accessibility tree and from focus). It handles focus, typing, selection, label association, and announcements. The canvas reads its state and draws it. If you don't need canvas specifically, styling the native input with CSS gets you the same look with far less risk.

## Markup and CSS

```html
<div class="field">
  <label for="email">Email address</label>
  <div class="control">
    <canvas aria-hidden="true"></canvas>
    <input id="email" type="email" autocomplete="email"
           placeholder="you@example.com" aria-describedby="email-error">
  </div>
  <p id="email-error" role="alert"></p>
</div>
```

```css
.control { position: relative; height: 44px; }
.control canvas,
.control input {
  position: absolute; inset: 0;
  width: 100%; height: 100%;
  box-sizing: border-box;
}
.control input {
  opacity: 0;               /* still focusable and exposed to AT */
  padding: 0 12px;          /* must match PADDING_X in JS */
  border: 0;
  font: 16px system-ui, sans-serif;  /* must match what the canvas draws */
}
```

## Rendering

```js
class CanvasTextField {
  constructor(input, canvas, opts = {}) {
    this.input = input;
    this.canvas = canvas;
    this.ctx = canvas.getContext('2d');
    this.padX = opts.paddingX ?? 12;
    this.colors = {
      bg: '#fff', border: '#6b6b6b', text: '#1a1a1a',
      placeholder: '#6b6b6b',               // keep ≥ 4.5:1 against bg
      selection: '#b3d4ff', caret: '#1a1a1a',
      focus: '#0b5fff', ...opts.colors,
    };
    this.caretVisible = true;
    this.reducedMotion = matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)').matches;

    const redraw = () => { this.resetBlink(); this.draw(); };
    for (const ev of ['input', 'keydown', 'keyup', 'select', 'click',
                      'mouseup', 'scroll', 'focus', 'blur'])
      input.addEventListener(ev, redraw);
    // keydown fires before the caret moves, so also check after the frame
    input.addEventListener('keydown', () => requestAnimationFrame(redraw));
    document.addEventListener('selectionchange', () => {
      if (document.activeElement === input) this.draw();
    });

    new ResizeObserver(() => this.resize()).observe(canvas);
    this.resize();
    this.startBlink();
  }

  resize() {
    const dpr = window.devicePixelRatio || 1;
    const { width, height } = this.canvas.getBoundingClientRect();
    this.canvas.width = Math.round(width * dpr);
    this.canvas.height = Math.round(height * dpr);
    this.ctx.setTransform(dpr, 0, 0, dpr, 0, 0);
    this.w = width; this.h = height;
    this.draw();
  }

  get font() {
    const s = getComputedStyle(this.input);
    return `${s.fontStyle} ${s.fontWeight} ${s.fontSize} ${s.fontFamily}`;
  }

  get displayValue() {
    const v = this.input.value;
    return this.input.type === 'password' ? '•'.repeat(v.length) : v;
  }

  startBlink() {
    if (this.reducedMotion) return;       // steady caret
    setInterval(() => {
      if (document.activeElement !== this.input) return;
      this.caretVisible = !this.caretVisible;
      this.draw();
    }, 530);
  }
  resetBlink() { this.caretVisible = true; }

  draw() {
    const { ctx, input, w, h, padX, colors } = this;
    const focused = document.activeElement === input;
    const text = this.displayValue;
    const scroll = input.scrollLeft;       // let the native input handle scrolling

    ctx.clearRect(0, 0, w, h);

    // Box
    ctx.fillStyle = colors.bg;
    ctx.strokeStyle = focused ? colors.focus : colors.border;
    ctx.lineWidth = focused ? 3 : 1;
    const r = 6, inset = ctx.lineWidth / 2;
    ctx.beginPath();
    ctx.roundRect(inset, inset, w - inset * 2, h - inset * 2, r);
    ctx.fill();
    ctx.stroke();

    // Clip text to the content area
    ctx.save();
    ctx.beginPath();
    ctx.rect(padX, 0, w - padX * 2, h);
    ctx.clip();

    ctx.font = this.font;
    ctx.textBaseline = 'middle';
    const midY = h / 2;
    const xAt = (i) => padX - scroll + ctx.measureText(text.slice(0, i)).width;

    // Selection
    const { selectionStart: a, selectionEnd: b, selectionDirection: dir } = input;
    if (focused && a !== b) {
      ctx.fillStyle = colors.selection;
      const x1 = xAt(a), x2 = xAt(b);
      ctx.fillRect(x1, 6, x2 - x1, h - 12);
    }

    // Text or placeholder
    if (text) {
      ctx.fillStyle = colors.text;
      ctx.fillText(text, padX - scroll, midY);
    } else if (input.placeholder) {
      ctx.fillStyle = colors.placeholder;
      ctx.fillText(input.placeholder, padX, midY);
    }

    // Caret
    if (focused && a === b && this.caretVisible) {
      const x = Math.round(xAt(a)) + 0.5;
      ctx.strokeStyle = colors.caret;
      ctx.lineWidth = 1;
      ctx.beginPath();
      ctx.moveTo(x, 10);
      ctx.lineTo(x, h - 10);
      ctx.stroke();
    }
    ctx.restore();
  }
}

const el = document.querySelector('#email');
new CanvasTextField(el, el.parentElement.querySelector('canvas'));
```

## Why this is built this way

- **Native input on top:** clicks, drag-selection, copy/paste, autofill, IME, and mobile keyboards all work natively. The canvas only mirrors `value`, `selectionStart/End`, and `scrollLeft`.
- **Matching metrics:** the input's font and padding must match what the canvas draws, or the native caret position and your drawn caret will drift apart.
- **Real label and errors:** use `<label for>` and `aria-describedby`, and put validation messages in real DOM (`role="alert"`), not on the canvas.
- **Reduced motion:** the caret stops blinking if the user asks for that.

## Known gaps to test

- **Forced-colors / Windows High Contrast:** canvas ignores it. You can use system color keywords (`CanvasText`, `Highlight`, `Field`) in `fillStyle` under `@media (forced-colors: active)`, but this needs explicit handling.
- **Browser zoom and text-only resize:** the canvas redraws on resize, but verify at 200–400% (WCAG 1.4.4 and 1.4.10).
- **Contrast:** the border, focus ring, and placeholder are all your responsibility (3:1 for the boundary and focus, 4.5:1 for text).
- **Right-to-left and complex scripts:** `measureText` on a prefix is wrong for bidi text and ligatures.
- **Real testing:** run it with NVDA, JAWS, VoiceOver (macOS and iOS), and TalkBack, and have your procurement or accessibility lead confirm what conformance evidence (VPAT/ACR) the contract needs.

If you'd like, I can write the same visual design as a pure CSS-styled native input. For a public-sector contract, that's the lower-risk path, and it also gives you autofill styling, forced-colors support, and zoom behavior for free.

